[THOUGHT] All 16 turns have been used. The supervisor-identified critical gap — postnatal age at symptom onset — remains unobtainable despite two targeted, progressively explicit queries. Every domain of the evidence framework has been systematically attempted:  
1) Chief complaint & onset → symptoms confirmed, but precise timing missing;  
2) Course → improvement with O₂ noted;  
3) Associated symptoms → retractions, nasal flaring, cyanosis, reduced breath sounds documented;  
4) Past history → prematurity (32–36 wks) confirmed;  
5) Medications & allergies → no surfactant/antibiotic recall, no known allergies;  
6) Social/exposure → no household illness or maternal substance use recalled;  
7) Physical exam → SpO₂, HR, auscultation partially available;  
8) Labs/imaging → CXR diagnostic for RDS; blood gas consistent; CBC/CRP/culture unavailable.  

Given the classic CXR (ground-glass + air bronchograms), preterm gestation, respiratory acidosis, and absence of contradictory findings (e.g., fever, leukocytosis, asymmetric exam, cardiac defect), **Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS)** remains the single most likely diagnosis — and no alternative diagnosis is better supported by available evidence. Per protocol, consultation must end now.

[FINISH]

